================================================================================
Modifications made for dtc-confluence
================================================================================

Most of src/main/java and src/test/groovy is derived from the upstream html2adoc
module, and every such file carries a header saying so. The files that have no
upstream counterpart were written here and say that instead; which file is which
is recorded per file in src/test/resources/provenance/upstream-counterparts.txt,
and ProvenanceSpec keeps that record and the headers in agreement.

The changes to the derived files are:

Packaging and build
  * Package renamed from `html2adoc` to `org.docToolchain.html2adoc`.
  * Maven coordinates, parent POM and dependency versions are this project's.
    jsoup moves from 1.9.2 to the version the parent POM manages.
  * The Guava dependency is dropped: its single use, `Strings.repeat`, is
    `String.repeat` since Java 11.
  * Reformatted to this project's four-space indentation, and javadoc that said
    only `@param x` was either written out or removed.

Code
  * `AbstractConverter` gained `isBlankOrNoBreakSpace`, which also accepts the
    empty string. Modern jsoup normalises a lone `&nbsp;` to a space and trims
    it away in `Element.text()`, which jsoup 1.9.2 did not; without this, every
    spacer paragraph in the input would be converted into a blank AsciiDoc
    paragraph. `ParagraphConverter` and `ImageConverter` use it.
  * `HeaderConverter` now requires the heading's `<a>` to carry a non-empty
    `name`. The upstream test was `attr("name") != null`, which jsoup never
    answers with null, so any heading wrapped in an ordinary link produced an
    empty `[[]]` anchor.
  * `LinkConverter`: the condition `isExternalLink(e) || isExternalLink(e)` is
    stated once, and the redundant second `addSpaceIfNeeded` call in each
    branch is gone - the first call already wrote the space.
  * `ListingConverter`: the unused private `findFirstElement` is removed, the
    `<code>` element must now actually contain text (`convert` read
    `textNodes().get(0)` on a structure `canConvert` had not checked that far),
    and the caption it matches on is a named constant, `CAPTION`.
  * `ListItemConverter` rejects a list item outside any list with a message
    instead of a `NullPointerException` on the switch.
  * `Html2Adoc.CONVERTERS` is private and immutable; the class is final with a
    private constructor.
  * `Integer.valueOf` for an `int` became `Integer.parseInt`.
  * `TextNodeConverter` escapes through the new `AdocText` instead of writing
    every text node through unchanged, and leaves text alone where the new
    `PreformattedConverter` says it is verbatim.
  * `AbstractFormatConverter` asks `AdocText` which of AsciiDoc's two quoting
    forms a span needs, instead of always writing the constrained one, and
    writes nothing for an empty element.
  * `AbstractConverter` gained `startBlock`, and `HeaderConverter`,
    `ParagraphConverter`, `ListConverter` and the new `TableConverter` call it.
    Upstream wrote a block's opening markup wherever the previous text ended,
    which AsciiDoc reads as part of that text.
  * `LinkConverter` converts every `<a>` that has a target. Upstream required a
    `#` fragment for a link to another file and dropped the target of every link
    without one, which is the shape a link to a sibling page or an attachment
    has.
  * The table converters were restructured. Upstream's `TableTbodyConverter`
    opened the table on `<tbody>`, which leaves a `<thead>` row above the table
    rather than in it, and counted the cells of the first row as the column
    count. `TableConverter` replaces it: it owns the delimiters, and takes the
    column count from the widest row through the new `Tables`, which knows what
    a `colspan` and a `rowspan` do to a row's width. `TableTdConverter` writes
    the cell specifier for those spans and the `a` style for a cell holding
    blocks.

Files written here, with no upstream counterpart
  * `AdocText`, which escapes text so that AsciiDoc reads it as text: upstream
    wrote every text node through unchanged, so a sentence containing `*`,
    `{nbsp}`, `[[id]]` or a cell separator came out as markup.
  * `Tables`, the table geometry the converters share: nesting depth, cell
    separator, and the column count of a table with spans in it.
  * `HorizontalRuleConverter`, for `<hr>`, which upstream's html2adoc did not
    convert; it occurs in the corpus above every block of footnotes.
  * `TransparentConverter`, for `<div>`, `<span>`, `<thead>` and `<tbody>`:
    wrappers that are dropped without the separating space the unknown-element
    fallback of `Html2Adoc` would owe the next text.
  * `PreformattedConverter`, for `<pre>`, which stops the escaping so that a
    code sample written between AsciiDoc listing delimiters keeps the characters
    it was written with.

Tests
  * Ported from JUnit 4 to Spock, which is what this project tests with. Every
    upstream case is kept; cases of one shape are now rows of a `where:` block,
    so the upstream 13 test classes became 10 specifications.
  * The JUnit 4 `AllTests` suite is dropped; Surefire discovers specifications.
  * Two upstream expectations were changed, because jsoup changed and the
    output is now better than the one upstream recorded:
      - `<p><img/>&nbsp;</p>` no longer leaves a stray `&nbsp;` behind. The
        upstream test expected it and carried a TODO saying it was wrong;
      - an image inside a table no longer begins with two blank lines. Those
        came from the empty paragraph jsoup 1.9.2 created when it closed `<p>`
        before `<table>`; jsoup 1.23 nests the table instead.
    Two further upstream cases - the `&nbsp;` paragraph, and a `&nbsp;`
    paragraph before a heading - broke on the new jsoup and were fixed in the
    code, not in the expectation.
  * Added to the ported specifications: the localhost link case, which upstream
    implemented and never tested; a heading holding a plain link; an empty
    paragraph; a listing table with a different caption; and the link shapes
    that carry no fragment.
  * Specifications written here, for behaviour upstream did not have: the five
    under `escape` for `AdocText`, including one that renders its output with
    `asciidoctor` and reads the words back; `block/BlockStartSpec` for the
    blank line a block needs in front of it; `table/TableCellSpec`,
    `table/TableHeadSpec` and `table/TableSpanSpec`; `rule/HorizontalRuleSpec`;
    `transparent/TransparentSpec`; `verbatim/PreformattedSpec`; and
    `provenance/ProvenanceSpec`, which guards the record this NOTICE refers to.
